{
  "result": "There is a particular kind of magic in an ordinary morning, the sort that disappears if you try to name it. Light slips through curtains, settles on the floor, and makes dust dance. A cup cools beside an unopened book. Somewhere, a bus sighs at the curb, and a bird repeats one bright note as though practicing for a concert no one has requested.\n\nThe world does not ask us to be impressive at these hours. It merely invites us to notice. We can watch steam vanish, feel the weight of a spoon, hear pipes ticking inside the walls. Such details seem small until we realize that a life is mostly built from them.\n\nAt noon, memory will probably blur this morning into a general shape: a little light, some quiet, the taste of coffee. Yet the unrecorded moments may be the ones that hold us together. They ask for no audience and earn no applause. They simply happen, offering their modest evidence that being alive is not always a grand adventure. Sometimes it is enough to stand at a window and witness the day becoming itself.\n\nWe forget this because ambition is loud. It teaches us to measure progress in milestones, titles, and finished lists. But attention keeps a different calendar. On it, a minute spent listening is not wasted, and a slow walk can be a destination. The heart, when unhurried, sees more than the hurried mind can carry.\n\nThat is why ordinary mornings deserve our gratitude, before they pass.",
